:: Start
# Lipid Naming Practice

Welcome! In this exercise you will be shown **six lipid structures** and asked to identify and name them using standard shorthand notation.

For each lipid, you will work through the structure **step by step**:
1. Identify the backbone
2. Identify the headgroup (where applicable)
3. Identify each fatty acid chain

The expected answer format is as per your lectures, for example: **PC(16:0/18:1(9Z))**

<div class="score-box">Your current score: <<print $score>> / 6</div>

---

[[Begin|Question 1]]

:: Question 1
<div class="step-label">Question 1 of 6</div>

## Identify this lipid

<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/yjRDld.png" alt="Lipid structure Q1">

//A glycerophospholipid with a glycerol backbone, a phosphate-linked headgroup, a saturated 16-carbon fatty acid at sn-1, and a monounsaturated 18-carbon fatty acid at sn-2 with a double bond at carbon 9 in the cis configuration.//

---

**Step 1: What is the backbone?**

[[A single fatty acid|Q1_Backbone_Wrong_FA]]
[[Glycerol|Q1_Backbone_Correct]]
[[Sphingosine|Q1_Backbone_Wrong_Sphingosine]]

:: Q1_Backbone_Wrong_Sphingosine
<div class="step-label">Question 1 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/yjRDld.png" alt="Lipid structure Q1">
<div class="feedback-wrong">❌ Not quite. Sphingosine is the backbone of sphingolipids — it contains a long-chain amino alcohol. Look again at the structure: there are three carbons with hydroxyl/ester linkages and no nitrogen on the backbone itself.</div>

[[Try again|Question 1]]

:: Q1_Backbone_Wrong_FA
<div class="step-label">Question 1 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/yjRDld.png" alt="Lipid structure Q1">
<div class="feedback-wrong">❌ Not quite. A single fatty acid would just be a carboxylic acid chain. This structure has a three-carbon backbone with multiple ester linkages.</div>

[[Try again|Question 1]]

:: Q1_Backbone_Correct
<div class="step-label">Question 1 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/yjRDld.png" alt="Lipid structure Q1">
<<set $q1_backbone to "correct">>
<div class="correct">✔ Correct! This is a glycerol backbone — the hallmark of glycerophospholipids and triacylglycerols.</div>

---

**Step 2: What is the headgroup attached to the phosphate?**

[[Choline|Q1_Headgroup_Correct]]
[[Ethanolamine|Q1_Headgroup_Wrong_PE]]
[[Inositol|Q1_Headgroup_Wrong_PI]]
[[Serine|Q1_Headgroup_Wrong_PS]]

:: Q1_Headgroup_Wrong_PE
<div class="step-label">Question 1 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/yjRDld.png" alt="Lipid structure Q1">
<div class="feedback-wrong">❌ Not quite. Ethanolamine is HO-CH₂-CH₂-NH₂ — it lacks the three methyl groups on the nitrogen. Look at the headgroup: does the nitrogen carry methyl groups?</div>

[[Try again|Q1_Backbone_Correct]]

:: Q1_Headgroup_Wrong_PS
<div class="step-label">Question 1 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/yjRDld.png" alt="Lipid structure Q1">
<div class="feedback-wrong">❌ Not quite. Serine carries an amino group AND a carboxyl group. The headgroup here is simpler than that.</div>

[[Try again|Q1_Backbone_Correct]]

:: Q1_Headgroup_Wrong_PI
<div class="step-label">Question 1 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/yjRDld.png" alt="Lipid structure Q1">
<div class="feedback-wrong">❌ Not quite. Inositol is a six-carbon ring with multiple hydroxyl groups — quite distinctive. The headgroup here is a small linear molecule.</div>

[[Try again|Q1_Backbone_Correct]]

:: Q1_Headgroup_Correct
<div class="step-label">Question 1 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/yjRDld.png" alt="Lipid structure Q1">
<<set $q1_headgroup to "correct">>
<div class="correct">✔ Correct! The headgroup is choline — (CH₃)₃N⁺-CH₂-CH₂-OH. This makes it a <strong>phosphatidylcholine (PC)</strong>.</div>

---

**Step 3: What is the fatty acid at sn-1?**

[[14:0 (Myristic acid)|Q1_SN1_Wrong_14]]
[[16:0 (Palmitic acid)|Q1_SN1_Correct]]
[[18:0 (Stearic acid)|Q1_SN1_Wrong_18]]
[[18:1(9Z) (Oleic acid)|Q1_SN1_Wrong_Oleic]]

:: Q1_SN1_Wrong_18
<div class="step-label">Question 1 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/yjRDld.png" alt="Lipid structure Q1">
<div class="feedback-wrong">❌ Not quite. Count the carbons in the sn-1 chain carefully — including the carbonyl carbon. Stearic acid has 18 carbons; this chain is shorter.</div>

[[Try again|Q1_Headgroup_Correct]]

:: Q1_SN1_Wrong_Oleic
<div class="step-label">Question 1 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/yjRDld.png" alt="Lipid structure Q1">
<div class="feedback-wrong">❌ Not quite. Oleic acid is 18 carbons and unsaturated. The sn-1 chain here is fully saturated — no double bonds — and has 16 carbons.</div>

[[Try again|Q1_Headgroup_Correct]]

:: Q1_SN1_Wrong_14
<div class="step-label">Question 1 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/yjRDld.png" alt="Lipid structure Q1">
<div class="feedback-wrong">❌ Not quite. Myristic acid has 14 carbons. Count again carefully from the carbonyl carbon.</div>

[[Try again|Q1_Headgroup_Correct]]

:: Q1_SN1_Correct
<div class="step-label">Question 1 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/yjRDld.png" alt="Lipid structure Q1">
<<set $q1_sn1 to "correct">>
<div class="correct">✔ Correct! The sn-1 fatty acid is <strong>palmitic acid — 16:0</strong>. Sixteen carbons, fully saturated.</div>

---

**Step 4: What is the fatty acid at sn-2?**

[[16:1(9Z) (Palmitoleic acid)|Q1_SN2_Wrong_16]]
[[18:0 (Stearic acid)|Q1_SN2_Wrong_Sat]]
[[18:1(9E) (Elaidic acid)|Q1_SN2_Wrong_Trans]]
[[18:1(9Z) (Oleic acid)|Q1_SN2_Correct]]

:: Q1_SN2_Wrong_Trans
<div class="step-label">Question 1 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/yjRDld.png" alt="Lipid structure Q1">
<div class="feedback-wrong">❌ Close — the carbon count and double bond position are right, but check the geometry. A <em>cis</em> double bond causes a pronounced kink in the chain. A <em>trans</em> double bond (9E) keeps the chain relatively straight. Which does this structure show?</div>

[[Try again|Q1_SN1_Correct]]

:: Q1_SN2_Wrong_Sat
<div class="step-label">Question 1 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/yjRDld.png" alt="Lipid structure Q1">
<div class="feedback-wrong">❌ Not quite. Look carefully at the sn-2 chain — there is a double bond present. A fully saturated chain would have no double bonds and would be written as 18:0.</div>

[[Try again|Q1_SN1_Correct]]

:: Q1_SN2_Wrong_16
<div class="step-label">Question 1 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/yjRDld.png" alt="Lipid structure Q1">
<div class="feedback-wrong">❌ Not quite. Count the carbons in the sn-2 chain again — including the carbonyl carbon. This chain has 18 carbons, not 16.</div>

[[Try again|Q1_SN1_Correct]]

:: Q1_SN2_Correct
<div class="step-label">Question 1 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/yjRDld.png" alt="Lipid structure Q1">
<<set $q1_sn2 to "correct">>
<<set $score to $score + 1>>
<div class="correct">✔ Correct! The sn-2 fatty acid is <strong>oleic acid — 18:1(9Z)</strong>. The double bond is between carbons 9 and 10, in the <em>cis</em> (Z) configuration.</div>

---

## ✅ Full name: PC(16:0/18:1(9Z))

This is **POPC** — 1-palmitoyl-2-oleoyl-sn-glycero-3-phosphocholine. One of the most abundant phospholipids in mammalian cell membranes.

<div class="score-box">Score so far: <<print $score>> / 6</div>

[[Continue|Question 2]]

:: Question 2
<div class="step-label">Question 2 of 6</div>

## Identify this lipid

<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/qKojPj.png" alt="Lipid structure Q2">

//A glycerophospholipid with a glycerol backbone, a phosphate-linked headgroup, a saturated 18-carbon fatty acid at sn-1, and a polyunsaturated 18-carbon fatty acid at sn-2 with two double bonds.//

---

**Step 1: What is the backbone?**

[[A single fatty acid|Q2_Backbone_Wrong_FA]]
[[Glycerol|Q2_Backbone_Correct]]
[[Sphingosine|Q2_Backbone_Wrong_Sphingosine]]

:: Q2_Backbone_Wrong_Sphingosine
<div class="step-label">Question 2 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/qKojPj.png" alt="Lipid structure Q2">
<div class="feedback-wrong">❌ Not quite. Look again — the backbone here has the same three-carbon glycerol structure you saw in Q1, with ester-linked fatty acids.</div>

[[Try again|Question 2]]

:: Q2_Backbone_Wrong_FA
<div class="step-label">Question 2 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/qKojPj.png" alt="Lipid structure Q2">
<div class="feedback-wrong">❌ Not quite. This structure has a three-carbon backbone with two fatty acid chains and a phosphate group attached.</div>

[[Try again|Question 2]]

:: Q2_Backbone_Correct
<div class="step-label">Question 2 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/qKojPj.png" alt="Lipid structure Q2">
<<set $q2_backbone to "correct">>
<div class="correct">✔ Correct! Glycerol backbone — another glycerophospholipid.</div>

---

**Step 2: What is the headgroup?**

[[Choline|Q2_Headgroup_Wrong_PC]]
[[Ethanolamine|Q2_Headgroup_Correct]]
[[Inositol|Q2_Headgroup_Wrong_PI]]
[[Serine|Q2_Headgroup_Wrong_PS]]

:: Q2_Headgroup_Wrong_PC
<div class="step-label">Question 2 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/qKojPj.png" alt="Lipid structure Q2">
<div class="feedback-wrong">❌ Not quite. Choline has a quaternary nitrogen — (CH₃)₃N⁺ — carrying three methyl groups. The headgroup here has a primary amine (NH₂) with no methyl groups. This is a common and important distinction!</div>

[[Try again|Q2_Backbone_Correct]]

:: Q2_Headgroup_Wrong_PS
<div class="step-label">Question 2 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/qKojPj.png" alt="Lipid structure Q2">
<div class="feedback-wrong">❌ Not quite. Serine has both an amino group and a carboxyl group. The headgroup here only has an amino group — simpler than serine.</div>

[[Try again|Q2_Backbone_Correct]]

:: Q2_Headgroup_Wrong_PI
<div class="step-label">Question 2 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/qKojPj.png" alt="Lipid structure Q2">
<div class="feedback-wrong">❌ Not quite. Inositol is a bulky six-membered ring with multiple -OH groups. The headgroup here is a small two-carbon molecule.</div>

[[Try again|Q2_Backbone_Correct]]

:: Q2_Headgroup_Correct
<div class="step-label">Question 2 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/qKojPj.png" alt="Lipid structure Q2">
<<set $q2_headgroup to "correct">>
<div class="correct">✔ Correct! The headgroup is ethanolamine — HO-CH₂-CH₂-NH₂. This makes it a <strong>phosphatidylethanolamine (PE)</strong>. Note the primary amine, versus the trimethylated nitrogen of choline in PC.</div>

---

**Step 3: What is the fatty acid at sn-1?**

[[16:0 (Palmitic acid)|Q2_SN1_Wrong_16]]
[[18:0 (Stearic acid)|Q2_SN1_Correct]]
[[18:1(9Z) (Oleic acid)|Q2_SN1_Wrong_Oleic]]
[[20:0 (Arachidic acid)|Q2_SN1_Wrong_20]]

:: Q2_SN1_Wrong_16
<div class="step-label">Question 2 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/qKojPj.png" alt="Lipid structure Q2">
<div class="feedback-wrong">❌ Not quite. Palmitic acid has 16 carbons. Count the carbons in the sn-1 chain again — this one is longer.</div>

[[Try again|Q2_Headgroup_Correct]]

:: Q2_SN1_Wrong_Oleic
<div class="step-label">Question 2 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/qKojPj.png" alt="Lipid structure Q2">
<div class="feedback-wrong">❌ Not quite. The sn-1 chain here is fully saturated — no double bonds. Oleic acid (18:1) has a double bond at carbon 9.</div>

[[Try again|Q2_Headgroup_Correct]]

:: Q2_SN1_Wrong_20
<div class="step-label">Question 2 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/qKojPj.png" alt="Lipid structure Q2">
<div class="feedback-wrong">❌ Not quite. Arachidic acid has 20 carbons. Count again — this chain has 18.</div>

[[Try again|Q2_Headgroup_Correct]]

:: Q2_SN1_Correct
<div class="step-label">Question 2 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/qKojPj.png" alt="Lipid structure Q2">
<<set $q2_sn1 to "correct">>
<div class="correct">✔ Correct! The sn-1 fatty acid is <strong>stearic acid — 18:0</strong>. Eighteen carbons, fully saturated.</div>

---

**Step 4: What is the fatty acid at sn-2?**

[[18:1(9Z) (Oleic acid)|Q2_SN2_Wrong_1DB]]
[[18:2(9Z,12Z) (Linoleic acid)|Q2_SN2_Correct]]
[[18:3(9Z,12Z,15Z) (α-Linolenic acid)|Q2_SN2_Wrong_3DB]]
[[20:4(5Z,8Z,11Z,14Z) (Arachidonic acid)|Q2_SN2_Wrong_AA]]

:: Q2_SN2_Wrong_3DB
<div class="step-label">Question 2 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/qKojPj.png" alt="Lipid structure Q2">
<div class="feedback-wrong">❌ Not quite. α-Linolenic acid has <em>three</em> double bonds. Count the double bonds in the sn-2 chain carefully — there are two.</div>

[[Try again|Q2_SN1_Correct]]

:: Q2_SN2_Wrong_1DB
<div class="step-label">Question 2 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/qKojPj.png" alt="Lipid structure Q2">
<div class="feedback-wrong">❌ Not quite. Oleic acid has only one double bond. Look at the sn-2 chain again — how many double bonds can you see?</div>

[[Try again|Q2_SN1_Correct]]

:: Q2_SN2_Wrong_AA
<div class="step-label">Question 2 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/qKojPj.png" alt="Lipid structure Q2">
<div class="feedback-wrong">❌ Not quite. Arachidonic acid has 20 carbons and four double bonds. The sn-2 chain here has 18 carbons and two double bonds.</div>

[[Try again|Q2_SN1_Correct]]

:: Q2_SN2_Correct
<div class="step-label">Question 2 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/qKojPj.png" alt="Lipid structure Q2">
<<set $q2_sn2 to "correct">>
<<set $score to $score + 1>>
<div class="correct">✔ Correct! The sn-2 fatty acid is <strong>linoleic acid — 18:2(9Z,12Z)</strong>. An essential omega-6 fatty acid.</div>

---

## ✅ Full name: PE(18:0/18:2(9Z,12Z))

A common phosphatidylethanolamine found in mammalian membranes. PE headgroups are smaller than PC, giving the lipid a more conical shape — important for membrane curvature.

<div class="score-box">Score so far: <<print $score>> / 6</div>

[[Continue|Question 3]]

:: Question 3
<div class="step-label">Question 3 of 6</div>

## Identify this lipid

<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/8KrBV0.png" alt="Lipid structure Q3">

//A glycerophospholipid with a glycerol backbone, a phosphate-linked headgroup, a saturated 18-carbon fatty acid at sn-1, and a polyunsaturated 20-carbon fatty acid at sn-2 with four double bonds.//

---

**Step 1: What is the backbone?**

[[A single fatty acid|Q3_Backbone_Wrong_FA]]
[[Glycerol|Q3_Backbone_Correct]]
[[Sphingosine|Q3_Backbone_Wrong_Sphingosine]]

:: Q3_Backbone_Wrong_Sphingosine
<div class="step-label">Question 3 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/8KrBV0.png" alt="Lipid structure Q3">
<div class="feedback-wrong">❌ Not quite. The backbone here is the same glycerol structure as Q1 and Q2.</div>

[[Try again|Question 3]]

:: Q3_Backbone_Wrong_FA
<div class="step-label">Question 3 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/8KrBV0.png" alt="Lipid structure Q3">
<div class="feedback-wrong">❌ Not quite. This structure has a three-carbon glycerol backbone with two ester-linked fatty acids and a phosphate group.</div>

[[Try again|Question 3]]

:: Q3_Backbone_Correct
<div class="step-label">Question 3 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/8KrBV0.png" alt="Lipid structure Q3">
<<set $q3_backbone to "correct">>
<div class="correct">✔ Correct! Glycerol backbone again.</div>

---

**Step 2: What is the headgroup?**

[[Choline|Q3_Headgroup_Wrong_PC]]
[[Ethanolamine|Q3_Headgroup_Wrong_PE]]
[[Inositol|Q3_Headgroup_Wrong_PI]]
[[Serine|Q3_Headgroup_Correct]]

:: Q3_Headgroup_Wrong_PC
<div class="step-label">Question 3 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/8KrBV0.png" alt="Lipid structure Q3">
<div class="feedback-wrong">❌ Not quite. Choline has a trimethylated nitrogen — (CH₃)₃N⁺. The headgroup here has both an amino group and a carboxyl group.</div>

[[Try again|Q3_Backbone_Correct]]

:: Q3_Headgroup_Wrong_PE
<div class="step-label">Question 3 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/8KrBV0.png" alt="Lipid structure Q3">
<div class="feedback-wrong">❌ Not quite. Ethanolamine is HO-CH₂-CH₂-NH₂ — just an amino group. The headgroup here also carries a carboxyl group, making it an amino acid.</div>

[[Try again|Q3_Backbone_Correct]]

:: Q3_Headgroup_Wrong_PI
<div class="step-label">Question 3 of 6</div>
<img class="lipid-image" src="https://i.vgy.me/8KrBV0.png" alt="Lipid structure Q3">
<div class="feedback-wrong">❌ Not quite. Inositol is a six-membered ring. The headgroup here is a small amino acid.</div>

[[Try again|Q3_Backbone_Correct]]

:: Q3_Headgroup_Correct
<div class="step-label">Question 3 of 6</div>
